    <h3 class="sub-title">Scores and editions in the analysis pages</h3>
    <ul class="about-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Bach fugues:</strong> engraved from David Huron’s Humdrum encoding of the Bach-Gesellschaft edition (BG 14; © 1994 David Huron, used for study); BWV 847 was
        also checked against Mutopia’s edition (CC BY-SA 4.0). Busoni’s edition (1894) and the Bach-Gesellschaft edition are in the public domain; the Neue Bach-Ausgabe
        urtext (Bärenreiter) was only compared.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>First Lessons in Bach:</strong> Walter Carroll’s 1911 pieces (Forsyth / Schuberth), re-engraved from his print.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Quantz and Blavet:</strong> the exercises are from Erwin Schwarz-Reiflingen’s edition of the <em>Flötenbuch</em> (Breitkopf 5606, 1934); the concerto
        follows Horst Augsbach’s edition (Breitkopf &amp; Härtel); Blavet’s sonata is engraved from a scan of the original print (Paris, 1731). Rules are paraphrased from
        Edward R. Reilly’s translation of Quantz (1966).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Japanese music guides:</strong> paraphrase Tokita and Hughes, <em>The Ashgate Research Companion to Japanese Music</em> (2017), with Malm, Miki and
        Kawase, all under their authors’ copyright, with short quotes and page citations. The sound-sample terms above apply to the samples only.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Dependencies keep their own licenses; Astro, tonal and VexFlow are MIT. Each page lists its own sources and credits.</p>

    <h2 class="section-title">Privacy</h2>
